<h2>Using page templates</h2>
<hr>
<p>Pages on pk-gov are generated by 11ty, based on several .njk templates. These templates handle things like
  adding relevant "boilerplate" elements such as print/share buttons and .hero sections. So, it is important
  that the correct template is used for each page. All "reusable" page templates (meaning they are used for
  more than one page) are located in the <code>'_includes/'</code> folder.
</p>

<h2>Types of page templates</h2>
<hr>
<h3>base.njk</h3>
<p>This is the .njk template that ALL pk-gov pages are based on. It contains regular HTML boilerplate as well
  as pk-gov's header and footer. General .css and .js files such as <code>variables.css</code>, <code>global.css</code>
  and <code>global.js</code> are included by default, with any additional files dynamically added when those
  files are passed in through the 'layoutCss' and 'layoutJs' properties in the front matter of templates, or
  the 'cssPath' and 'jsPath' properties in the front matter of individual pages.
</p>
<p>As this is the base layout, it is recommended to avoid directly using base.njk as the layout for a page,
  unless the other templates do not suit the desired design e.g. having unnecessary elements.
</p>

<h3>content-page.njk</h3>
<p>This is used by all 'content pages', such as Government news articles and pk-dev pages. It applies 
  <code>content-page.css</code> by default, and includes a .hero section, table of contents and 'end-buttons'
  consisting of a print, share and .return button, which are automatically adjusted based on the tag used in
  a page's front matter. Additional .css and .js files can be addded to the 'cssPath' and 'jsPath' properties
  in the front matter of individual pages.
</p>
<p>This template is recommended for content-heavy pages where there is a lot of text, and minimal interactive
  elements such as forms and buttons are required. Pages using this template are recommended to be written in
  Markdown for easier editing (though pk-dev pages use HTML in .njk as many non-standard components have to be
  included in pages).
</p>

<h3>ministry.njk</h3>
<p>This is used by all ministry pages. It applies <code>ministries.css</code> and <code>ministries.js</code>
by default, and injects the fileSlug name of the current ministry page into <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> so
that the correct lottie animation can be played. It also includes a .return button at the end of the page.
</p>
<p>This template should ONLY be used for ministry pages, meaning it is essentially not needed for any future
  content.
</p>

<h3>service.njk</h3>
<p>This is used by all services (as in the actual service pages, the Start Pages use <code>content-page.njk</code>).
  It applies <code>content-page.css</code> by default, with additional .css and .js files addded through the
  'cssPath' and 'jsPath' properties in the front matter of individual pages. It does not contain any additional
  page elements, acting as a 'blank slate' as services generally vary more in layout compared to content pages.
</p>
<p>This template should ONLY be used for service pages, as it provides the flexibility for non-standard layouts
  and applies <code>content-page.css</code> for basic styling of content. Pages that are not services but do
  not fit the layout of <code>content-page.njk</code> should use <code>base.njk</code> as their template.
</p>